window.docContents[172].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-debugging-greedy":{"contents":"Lean provides the debugging tool dbg_trace: the expression dbg_trace \"msg\"; e\nevaluates to e, printing msg as a side effect each time it is evaluated. We make\na version of greedy that prints a * each time it is called:\n\nA copy of greedy that prints one * per call./-- A copy of greedy that prints one * per call. -/\ndef greedyTraced : ℕ → ℕ :=\n  fun v =>\n    dbg_trace \"*\";\n    G.greedyChoice (fun u ↦ if u < v then greedyTraced u else 0) v\n\n\nRather than printing thousands of stars and asking you to count them, we can capture\nthe trace inside Lean: IO.FS.withIsolatedStreams runs an IO action with its\noutput redirected into a String.\n\nEvaluate f (), capturing everything it dbg_traces; return the value together\nwith the number of trace lines (here: the number of calls)./-- Evaluate f (), capturing everything it dbg_traces; return the value together\nwith the number of trace lines (here: the number of calls). -/\ndef withTraceCount {α : Type} (f : Unit → α) : IO (α × ℕ) := do\n  let (out, a) ← IO.FS.withIsolatedStreams (IO.lazyPure f)\n  -- each trace ends in a newline, so splitOn gives one more piece than there\n  -- are traces\n  return (a, (out.splitOn \"\\n\").length - 1)\n\n\nBefore looking at the trace counts below, can you work out how bad it is?\n\n#eval do -- [1, 3, 13, 69, 431, 3103, 25341, 231689, 2345851]\n  -- returns the number of calls made by greedy for\n  -- the complete graph on ℕ when evaluated at 0, 1, ..., 8\n  let counts ← (List.range 9).mapM fun n =>\n    withTraceCount fun _ => (⊤ : SimpleGraph ℕ).greedyTraced n\n  return counts.map Prod.snd\n\n\nSo colouring vertex 8 of the complete graph, where 8 has eight earlier\nneighbours, takes a truly unreasonable 2,345,851 calls! Even on the path\npathNat₁, where every vertex has at most one earlier neighbour, colouring vertex\n8 takes 46 calls. Why is it so bad?\n\nFirstly, nothing is stored: each time the algorithm needs the colour of an earlier\nvertex m, it recomputes greedy m completely from scratch. Secondly, Nat.find\nfinds the least valid colour by testing 0, 1, 2, ... in turn, and every test\nrecomputes the finset (G.neighborLTFinset v).image c, thereby recomputing the\ncolour of every earlier neighbour of v.\n\nWriting C(n) for the number of calls made when colouring n, and c(n) for the\ncolour that is chosen, we have C(n) = 1 + (c(n) + 1) * ∑ C(m), where the sum runs\nover all earlier neighbours m of n (there are c(n) + 1 tests, each recomputing\nthe image). On the complete graph ⊤ we have c(n) = n, and so\nC(n) = 1 + (n + 1) * (C(0) + C(1) + ⋯ + C(n - 1)), which grows faster than n!.\n(On the path the colours alternate between 0 and 1, and C(n) grows like\n2 ^ (n / 2).)\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Greedy Colouring*\u0009Making  greedy  Fast*","header":"7.8.6.1. Debugging  greedy","id":"/Colorings/#section-debugging-greedy"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-cardinality":{"contents":"Counting crops up everywhere in graph theory: the number of vertices, the number of\nedges, the degree of a vertex (the number of its neighbours), the size of a clique,\nand so on. Mathlib offers several different notions of cardinality, and which one\nto use depends on what kind of object we are counting and what finiteness information\nwe have available. We describe the main ones below.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Finite Types","header":"4.2.4. Cardinality","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-cardinality"},"/Subgraphs/#section-spanning-subgraphs":{"contents":"A subgraph H : G.Subgraph is spanning if H.verts = Set.univ, i.e. it contains\nevery vertex in V.\n\nA graph G, a spanning subgraph, and a non-spanning subgraph\n\nexample (H : G.Subgraph) : H.IsSpanning ↔ H.verts = Set.univ := isSpanning_iff\n\n\nIf G H : SimpleGraph V and h : H ≤ G then toSubgraph H h is a spanning subgraph\nof G.\n\nexample (H : SimpleGraph V) (h : H ≤ G) : (toSubgraph H h).IsSpanning :=\n  toSubgraph.isSpanning _ _\n\n\nIf we take the spanningCoe of a toSubgraph then we get back the original\nSimpleGraph V.\n\nexample (H : SimpleGraph V) (h : H ≤ G) : (toSubgraph H h).spanningCoe = H := rfl\n\n\nThe subgraph ⊤ is spanning.\n\nexample : (⊤ : G.Subgraph).IsSpanning := fun _ => trivial\n\n\nThe spanningCoe of the G.Subgraph consisting of a single edge is the\nSimpleGraph V constructed from the edge set containing this single edge.\n\nexample {v w : V} (hadj : G.Adj v w) :\n    (G.subgraphOfAdj hadj).spanningCoe = fromEdgeSet {s(v, w)} :=\n  Subgraph.spanningCoe_subgraphOfAdj hadj\n\n\nIf H : G.Subgraph then there is a graph embedding of H.coe into H.spanningCoe.\n\n#check coeEmbeddingSpanningCoe\n\n\nIf H : G.Subgraph is a spanning subgraph then H.spanningCoe and H.coe are\nisomorphic. (They cannot be equal, since the former is a SimpleGraph V while the\nlatter is a SimpleGraph ↑H.verts where H.verts = Set.univ.)\n\nexample (H : G.Subgraph) (h : H.IsSpanning) :\n    H.spanningCoe ≃g H.coe := H.spanningCoeEquivCoeOfSpanning h\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Subgraphs as Simple Graphs","header":"9.3.2. Spanning Subgraphs","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-spanning-subgraphs"}});